<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Creek War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Red Stick War · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> War of 1813 to 1814 began as a civil war among the Muscogee between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sticks">Red Sticks</a>, who resisted American expansion, and those friendly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. American militia and regulars under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> and Lower Muscogee allies, crushed the Red Sticks at Horseshoe Bend in March 1814. The Treaty of Fort Jackson forced the Muscogee to cede some 23 million acres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/creek-war.jpg" alt="Creek War" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Reuben Chapin, 1823-1894 , artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jackson_and_Weatherford.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
